// **the ownership half of reading a borrow-returning call, and the one that fails silently.**
//
// the deref AST::PointerAdjuster writes is only one of four answers to "does this read reach
// storage". AST::OwnershipPass owes another: a read that reaches storage is *copied*, because what it
// found still belongs to whoever named it, while a value the program computed is moved. a call used to
// answer "computed" at that question, so a value read of an owning element was a bitwise copy of the
// element's handle with no copy constructor - and then two owners of one buffer, freed twice
//
// the element is an `array<int32>` rather than a `string`, and that choice is the test: `string`
// declares no destructor deliberately, so a missing copy of one is invisible. an inner array owns a
// buffer and destroys it, so a shared handle is a double free at the two scope exits
//
// **the allocation counts are the assertion.** the echoes below pass either way - reassigning a
// shared handle does not mutate what it shared - so what says the copy constructor ran is the fourth
// buffer appearing and going away again

array<array<int32>> $rows = [];

$rows->push([1, 2, 3]);
$rows->push([4, 5]);

// the outer buffer and one per row
assert(mem::live_allocations() == 3, "one buffer for the rows and one per row");

{
    array<int32> $copy = $rows->at(0);

    // **the fourth buffer is the whole point.** three here means the copy shared the row's storage
    assert(mem::live_allocations() == 4, "the copy owns a buffer of its own");

    // and it is independent: growing the copy does not grow the row
    $copy->push(99);

    echo $copy->count();
    echo $rows[0]->count();
}

// the copy gave its buffer back, and the row still has its own
assert(mem::live_allocations() == 3, "the copy is gone, the rows are not");

echo $rows[0]->count();
echo $rows->at(1)->count();
